what the windows installer carries
----------------------------------

velvetdesk ships a piece of gstreamer, twenty eight files and fourteen megabytes, taken
unmodified from the official windows build. it is the subset the screen share loads and
nothing more, relevee by running the pipelines and reading the process' modules; the list
lives in `tools/gstreamer-embarque.ps1`, which is also what puts it in place.

    gstreamer (core, base, good, bad)                          LGPL-2.1-or-later

it is there because windows graphics capture, the thing that lets you share one window
rather than the rectangle of desktop it sits on, is a winrt api. ffmpeg does not expose it
and the jvm cannot call it. gstreamer does, and velvet does not link against it: it runs
`gst-launch-1.0` as a separate process and reads raw frames from its output.

LGPL asks that the library stay replaceable, and it does. the files sit beside the
installed application, in their own directory, and velvet uses whatever is there. sources
and the licence text: https://gstreamer.freedesktop.org/


what this repository deliberately does not contain
--------------------------------------------------

`gg sans`, discord's interface font. four weights are looked up by name in
`PoliceAndroid.kt` and by the desktop resource loader, and none of the four files is here:
the font belongs to discord and is redistributable under no condition. both sides resolve
them at runtime and fall back to the system font when they are absent, saying so in the log,
so a fresh clone builds and runs — it simply does not look like the reference until someone
drops their own copy into `res/font/` and `resources/polices/`. the free substitution is
named in `PoliceAndroid.kt`: inter, under the SIL open font licence.

discord's shop artwork — the avatar decorations, profile effects and nameplates the app can
wear. they are downloaded by the owner of a homeserver into a catalogue room, they are not
ours to hand on, and the scripts that fetch them are not published either.

discord's interface itself. the layout, the spacing and the gestures were measured on
screenshots and rebuilt; no asset, no code and no font was taken. the screenshots are not
in this repository.

the sound effects (joining and leaving a call, mute, deafen, the ringtone). where they came
from was never written down, so they stay out. both apps look them up by name and play
nothing when they are missing; drop your own .ogg files in `res/raw/` and
`resources/sons/` under the names the code asks for.

anything belonging to the people who run this. no conversation, no capture, no working
document, no name. that is on purpose rather than by oversight.


on the network clause
---------------------

the AGPL's section 13 is the reason it was chosen over the GPL. this is a chat server as
much as it is an app: if you run a modified velvet for other people to talk on, they get to
have your changes. that is the whole bargain, and it is the one that keeps a house from
turning into somebody's product.
